## 2. Step-by-Step System Testing Using Concurrency Models
Let’s apply Combinatorics and Probability Theory to model a Registration Voucher Race Condition outside an account.
The Scenario: A developer wants to prevent promotional voucher abuse. When an unauthenticated visitor registers with a sign-up code (?code=YOURCODE), the backend executes three discrete relational operations:

$is_used = check_database_if_code_used($code); // Operation 1 (Read)
if (!$is_used) {
    credit_welcome_bonus($user_id);            // Operation 2 (Write/Credit)
    mark_code_as_used($code);                  // Operation 3 (Update/Lock)
}

## The Concurrency Gap (Δ t)
Let Δ t be the time interval (in milliseconds) between Operation 1 (Read) and Operation 3 (Update/Lock). During this specific window, the code is structurally blind because the database has checked the token but has not yet updated its state to "used."
If an attacker sends n identical registration requests simultaneously across a cluster of network threads, the arrival of these requests behaves like an independent Poisson process.
## The Mathematical Proof of Duplication
Let our target state be X ≥ 2 (meaning two or more requests bypass the validation check inside the same Δ t interval before the lock updates). The probability of a successful exploit is modeled as:
$$P(X \ge 2) = 1 - P(X=0) - P(X=1) = 1 - e^{-\lambda} - \lambda e^{-\lambda}$$ 
Where λ = n × Δ t represents request density.
As an investigator, you manipulate this equation by maximizing λ. If a normal web script sends 1 request at a time with a latency variance of 50ms, λ stays near 0, meaning the probability of a collision is mathematically impossible:
$$P(X \ge 2) \approx 0$$ 
To execute a successful proof-of-concept, you must force λ to scale drastically. You do this by utilizing a Single Packet Attack framework. By packaging 50 HTTP requests (n=50) into a single TCP frame window, you compress the arrival variance (Δ t) down to less than 1 millisecond.
Substituting these values shifts the probability distribution graph:
$$\lambda = 50 \times 0.01 = 0.5 \implies P(X \ge 2) \approx 0.0902 \quad (9\% \text{ success rate per frame})$$ 
By repeating this packet layout over a sequence of 10 structural windows, the cumulative probability of duplicating the starting balance approaches certainty:
$$P(\text{Success}) = 1 - (1 - 0.0902)^{10} \approx 0.612 \quad (61.2\%)$$ 
